<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  StochCraft's vendor extensions to BPSim 2.0 and 1.0 (MIT OR Apache-2.0).

  BPSim's VendorExtension takes a `name` and, for its value, attributes and elements from another
  namespace, which a validator checks strictly: this schema declares StochCraft's. Validate with
  it imported next to the BPSim schema.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:queueCapacity"
        xmlns:stochcraft="https://helenkwok.github.io/StochCraft/bpsim/1" stochcraft:value="3"/>

  stochcraft:queueCapacity, on a task's ElementParameters: the most entities that wait for the
  task's resource (not counting those in service), a whole number of at least 0. By default the
  task's waiting room has a place for every unit of its resource plus this many, an entity
  holding one from entering until it leaves.

  Two other ways set a limit on the number waiting that changes over time, and hold no places:
  an entity is admitted when those waiting (in the queue, or on their way in across a transfer)
  less the idle working units are fewer than the limit at that moment, so it gets in when an idle
  working unit can take it at once, or when fewer than the limit wait. Working is up, not taken
  out by a health check and on shift; an entity whose job is paused on its unit is in service.
  When the limit drops, those already waiting stay. With perWorkingServer="true", the limit is
  the value times the units working now, so it follows failures, health checks, shifts and
  returns by itself. With a byShift element holding BPSim values validFor calendars (as a
  resource's quantity by shift), the limit is the value of the first calendar active, and the
  value outside them. Versions of StochCraft without these read the value alone, as a fixed
  waiting room.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:queueCapacity" xmlns:stochcraft="…" stochcraft:value="1">
      <stochcraft:byShift>
        <bpsim:NumericParameter value="6" validFor="afternoon"/>
      </stochcraft:byShift>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:queueCapacity" xmlns:stochcraft="…"
        stochcraft:value="2" stochcraft:perWorkingServer="true"/>

  stochcraft:pool, on a resource's ElementParameters, with the value true: the resource is a
  shared pool, even when one task uses it or none. Without it, a resource that one task needs a
  single unit of may be read as that task's own resource, unless its quantity has values
  validFor a calendar. With the value false, such a resource is that task's own even then: the
  task's units follow its shifts.

  stochcraft:batchSize, on a task's ElementParameters: the task collects this many entities
  (a whole number of at least 1) and sends them on together as one batch.

  stochcraft:unbatch, on a task's ElementParameters, with the value true: the task splits each
  batch back into its entities.

  stochcraft:quantity, on the ElementParameters of a sequence flow into a parallel gateway: how
  many entities from that flow go into each one the gateway sends on (a bill of materials;
  a whole number of at least 1).

  stochcraft:breakdown, on a task's ElementParameters: its resource's units fail after a time
  drawn from timeToFailure (elapsed since the last repair) and are repaired for a time drawn from
  repairTime, each holding one BPSim distribution or constant. Whether a failure interrupts the
  job under way is BPSim's own PriorityParameters/Interruptible. With handsOnWhileDown="false", a
  unit that fails while holding a finished part keeps it until repaired (by default it hands it
  on as soon as there is room).

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:breakdown" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:breakdown>
        <stochcraft:timeToFailure><bpsim:ExponentialDistribution mean="480"/></stochcraft:timeToFailure>
        <stochcraft:repairTime><bpsim:TriangularDistribution min="10" mode="20" max="60"/></stochcraft:repairTime>
      </stochcraft:breakdown>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:transferTime, on a task's ElementParameters: the time a part takes to reach the
  task from the element that sends it, holding one BPSim distribution or constant. The part
  takes its place in the task's buffer (queueCapacity) when the transfer starts and joins the
  queue when it ends; a sending task's resource stays occupied through the transfer, unless the
  attribute holdsSender is false: then it is free at once, and the time is a minimum time in the
  buffer.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:transferTime" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:transferTime holdsSender="false"><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="32"/></stochcraft:transferTime>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:rework, on a sequence flow's ElementParameters, with the value true: entities
  taking it failed their quality requirements and go back to be worked on again (ISO 22400-2's
  rework quantity), counted against the last task with a resource of its own they finished at.

  stochcraft:patience, on a task's ElementParameters: the time an entity waits at the task before
  it gives up (reneging), drawn for each entity as it enters (before any transfer), holding one
  BPSim distribution or constant. With the attribute until="start" (the default) it gives up if
  no unit of the task's resource has taken it by then; with until="done", also if its service is
  not done by then (the unit finishes the job anyway, and the work is wasted). An entity that
  gives up takes the way out marked stochcraft:abandoned, or leaves the model. BPSim 2.0 has no
  parameter for this: its queueTime and waitTime are times every entity spends (they make up its
  lagTime), and a boundary timer's interTriggerTimer is a repeating interval, not a deadline
  drawn for each entity.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:patience" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:patience until="done"><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="30"/></stochcraft:patience>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:balking, on a task's ElementParameters: an entity arriving at the task looks at its
  queue and joins with a chance that depends on the number n it finds there; otherwise it balks
  (it never enters, and takes the way out marked stochcraft:balked, or leaves the model). n counts
  those waiting (in the queue, or in transfer to the task), or with countsInService="true" also
  those in service. With rule="table" (the default) the chance is given by stochcraft:join rows,
  in increasing order of waiting: from each row's number on, its probability; below the first
  row, 1. With rule="reciprocal" it is 1/(n + 1). BPSim 2.0 has no parameter for it.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:balking" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:balking>
        <stochcraft:join waiting="2" probability="0.5"/>
        <stochcraft:join waiting="5" probability="0"/>
      </stochcraft:balking>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:healthCheck, on a task's ElementParameters: each unit of the task's resource is
  checked every interval (its own checks, the first one interval after the start) and fails a
  check when more entities wait at the task than limit per unit working (not failed, not out and
  on shift); a unit that fails is out of service for the restart time, then comes back. The
  optional keep is the fewest units left working: a failed check takes no unit out below it.
  onTakeout="retry" (the default) sends a job under way on a unit taken out back to the head of
  the queue at once (its work is wasted); onTakeout="resume" lets it wait on the unit and resume
  after the restart. With a finite waiting room, an entity keeps its place throughout (back in
  the queue, or waiting on its unit); placeWhileOut="reserved" (the default) has a unit taken out
  keep a place for itself while it restarts, as a failed unit does: once the job under way (if
  any) is back in the queue, it takes a place that is free at that moment, if one is, and gives
  it back when its restart ends, so the buffer holds fewer while units are out.
  placeWhileOut="open" has it hold none, as a unit off shift. With an unlimited waiting room it
  changes nothing. BPSim 2.0 has nothing for it.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:healthCheck" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:healthCheck limit="2" keep="3">
        <stochcraft:interval><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="10"/></stochcraft:interval>
        <stochcraft:restart><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="60"/></stochcraft:restart>
      </stochcraft:healthCheck>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:onShiftEnd, on a task's ElementParameters, with the value finish, retry or resume:
  what happens to a job under way on a unit of the task's own resource that goes off shift (the
  units on shift drop, by the resource's quantity validFor a calendar, or its availability
  ends). finish (the default) lets the unit finish the job, then go off; retry sends it back to
  the head of the queue at once (its work is wasted); resume lets it wait on the unit and carry
  on with what was left when the unit is on shift again. Units go off idle ones first, then
  failed or restarting ones, then busy ones, the job started last first.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:onShiftEnd"
        xmlns:stochcraft="https://helenkwok.github.io/StochCraft/bpsim/1" stochcraft:value="retry"/>

  stochcraft:abandoned, on a sequence flow's ElementParameters, with the value true: the way out
  of a task with a patience that entities giving up take (a retry, a call back later), besides
  the task's ordinary way out.

  stochcraft:turnedAway, on a sequence flow's ElementParameters, with the value true: the way out
  of a task with a queueCapacity that entities finding no room take at once (to try again later,
  or go elsewhere), besides the task's ordinary way out. Without it, an arrival from a start
  event finding no room is lost and an entity from any other element waits for room before the
  task; with it, both take this way, counted as lost by the task (and by the start event, for
  its arrivals). At most one per task.

  stochcraft:balked, on a sequence flow's ElementParameters, with the value true: the way out of
  a task with stochcraft:balking that entities not joining its queue take at once (to try again
  later, or go elsewhere), besides the task's ordinary way out. At most one per task.

  stochcraft:scrap, on an end event's ElementParameters: what reaches it is scrap (ISO
  22400-2's scrap quantity), counted against the task the attribute chargedTo names (an
  inspection rejecting work done upstream), or else the last task with a resource of its own
  each entity finished at.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:scrap" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:scrap chargedTo="machine"/>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:demand, on an end event's ElementParameters: the customer demand it serves, for
  takt (the available time divided by the demand over it): quantity parts per run (the measured
  window), day, week or shift. With calendar, only the time that calendar is active counts as
  available, and with per="shift" each active period of it is a shift (needed then).

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:demand" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:demand quantity="480" per="shift" calendar="day-shift"/>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:plannedRunTime, on a task's ElementParameters: the planned time for the task's
  resource to make one item (ISO 22400-2's planned runtime per item), holding one BPSim
  distribution or constant whose mean is the plan. It is not simulated; results use it for
  effectiveness and OEE.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:plannedRunTime" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:plannedRunTime><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="30"/></stochcraft:plannedRunTime>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:changeover, on a task's ElementParameters: its resource's unit sets up before a part
  only when the part's value of the token property named by the attribute property differs from
  that of the part the unit served last (its first part needs none). The setup takes the time of
  the stochcraft:change whose from and to are the two values, else stochcraft:time, each holding
  one BPSim distribution or constant. A setup before every job is BPSim's own
  TimeParameters/SetUpTime.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:changeover" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:changeover property="type">
        <stochcraft:time><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="10"/></stochcraft:time>
        <stochcraft:change from="A" to="B"><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="25"/></stochcraft:change>
      </stochcraft:changeover>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:processingTimeBy, on a task's ElementParameters: processing times by the value of
  the token property named by the attribute property (a time by product type). A token whose
  value, as text, is the value of a stochcraft:time takes that time, a BPSim distribution or
  constant; any other token (another value, or none) takes the task's own
  TimeParameters/ProcessingTime. Each value appears once.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:processingTimeBy" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:processingTimeBy property="type">
        <stochcraft:time value="A"><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="6"/></stochcraft:time>
        <stochcraft:time value="B"><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="20"/></stochcraft:time>
      </stochcraft:processingTimeBy>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:processingTimeByResource, on a task's ElementParameters, for a task with shared
  resources (a selection naming pools): processing times by the pool whose units serve the
  token, for servers that differ in speed. A token served by units of the pool named by the
  attribute resource of a stochcraft:time takes that time, a BPSim distribution or constant,
  ahead of the task's stochcraft:processingTimeBy and its own TimeParameters/ProcessingTime;
  a take that spans pools counts the pool that supplied its first unit. Each pool appears once
  and is one the task needs.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:processingTimeByResource" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:processingTimeByResource>
        <stochcraft:time resource="able"><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="2.5"/></stochcraft:time>
        <stochcraft:time resource="baker"><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="4"/></stochcraft:time>
      </stochcraft:processingTimeByResource>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:routing, on an exclusive gateway's ElementParameters, with the value shortestQueue
  (or standard, BPMN's own routing): each entity goes to the way with the fewest entities waiting
  or in transit among those with room, a tie to the way taken longest ago; while no way has
  room, the element sending into the gateway waits (a task's resource stays blocked) and a
  start event's arrival is lost.

  stochcraft:power, on a task's ElementParameters: the power each unit of the task's resource
  draws in each state, in kilowatts (numbers of at least 0): processing and idle (both needed),
  and blocked, failed and offShift, each idle when left out.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:power" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:power processing="12.5" idle="2" failed="0.5" offShift="0"/>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:conveyor, on a task's ElementParameters: the task is a conveyor. length, speed
  (the length travelled per base time unit), itemLength (above 0) and gap (0 or more) are in
  the scenario's length unit; every item takes a slot of itemLength + gap. accumulating says
  whether items stop behind a stopped one ahead (true) or the whole belt stops (false). shape is
  straight (the default), curve (with radius and angle in degrees) or spiral (with radius, rise
  per turn, and turns), for drawing only. Other tools see a plain task.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:conveyor" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:conveyor length="12.5" speed="0.5" accumulating="true" itemLength="0.8" gap="0.2"/>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:lengthUnit, on the scenario's ScenarioParameters, with the value mm, cm, m, km, in
  or ft: the unit lengths are in (metres without it).

  stochcraft:layoutScale, on the scenario's ScenarioParameters, with a number above 0 as its
  value: the length units per drawing unit of the diagram (1 without it). The distance between
  two elements is the straight line between the centres of their shapes, times this.

  stochcraft:transport, on a task's ElementParameters: the task carries each item from the
  element before it to the element after it with a unit of the mobile resource `pool`. The unit
  loads for a time drawn from load and unloads for one drawn from unload. Other tools see a
  plain task.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:transport" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:transport pool="operators">
        <stochcraft:load><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="1"/></stochcraft:load>
        <stochcraft:unload><bpsim:FloatingParameter value="1"/></stochcraft:unload>
      </stochcraft:transport>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:mobility, on a resource's ElementParameters (with stochcraft:pool): its units move.
  speedEmpty and speedLoaded (above 0) are in the scenario's length unit per base time unit;
  home is the id of the element the units start at; afterJob is stay (where the unit put the
  item down) or goHome.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:mobility" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:mobility speedEmpty="1" speedLoaded="2" home="store" afterJob="stay"/>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:stopCount, on an element's ElementParameters: each replication ends once this many
  entities (a whole number of at least 1) have left the element since time 0 (for an end event:
  arrived there), or at the scenario's duration if that comes first.

  stochcraft:stopWhenEmpty, on a task's ElementParameters, with the value true: each replication
  ends the first time the task holds no entity, none waiting and none in service, after it has
  taken one in.

  stochcraft:estimate, on the ElementParameters of an element with a time distribution (a start
  event's interTriggerTimer, a task's processingTime): the three-point estimate the distribution
  was fitted from, kept as its pedigree. The distribution itself is the standard BPSim one in the
  parameters; this records where it came from. Exactly one of max and p95.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:estimate" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:estimate family="pert" min="10" mode="20" max="60" source="Delphi, round 2"/>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:sequential, on the scenario's ScenarioParameters: more replications are run until
  every watched result is precise enough, or max replications have run. relative (a share of the
  mean), points (a share's half-width in percentage points) and max (a whole number of at
  least 1) are the targets; element and metric, together, watch one result, with an optional
  halfWidth in its own unit.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:sequential" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:sequential relative="0.05" points="1" max="200"/>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:observed, on a Scenario (after its Calendars): real measurements the results are
  checked against. Each observation names a result, by element (a block or pool id) and metric
  (its name in the results), and gives either value (the observed value, with the optional
  halfWidth of its own 95% interval, and an optional tolerance above 0, the difference that still
  counts as agreeing) or a bound, atMost or atLeast, in the metric's own unit (a
  share as a fraction). An observed value differs from the simulated mean when the 95% interval of
  their difference excludes 0 (with a tolerance: lies wholly outside it), and agrees only when
  that interval lies wholly inside the tolerance; a bound is decided by the simulated 95%
  interval. A scenario that
  inherits another has its own observations or none: stochcraft:value="off" clears what the
  inherited scenario sets.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:observed" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:observed>
        <stochcraft:observation element="desk" metric="mean_wait" value="3.2" halfWidth="0.4" tolerance="0.5"/>
        <stochcraft:observation element="desk" metric="utilisation" atMost="0.85"/>
      </stochcraft:observed>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:headline, on a Scenario (after its ElementParameters and Calendars): the one result
  the model's question asks for, shown first among the results. name says what it is, in the
  model's own words; each term names a result, by element (a block id) and metric (its name in
  the results). The terms are added up in each replication and the sum summarised across them.
  A scenario that inherits another inherits it, or replaces it with its own.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:headline" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:headline name="Patients fit to leave still in hospital">
        <stochcraft:term element="hospital_nursing_home" metric="mean_contents"/>
        <stochcraft:term element="hospital_care_at_home" metric="mean_contents"/>
      </stochcraft:headline>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:monteCarlo, on a Scenario (after its ElementParameters and Calendars): the scenario
  is a Monte Carlo model, a calculation with no process, and the file needs none. Its inputs are
  the scenario's own properties (ScenarioParameters/PropertyParameters/Property, each a BPSim
  distribution or a number); each trial draws every input once and evaluates every output in
  order, and the scenario's replication is the number of trials. sampling is random (the
  default), latinHypercube, antithetic or sobol. input gives an input's unit; output an expression (BPSim's XPath
  subset, reading inputs and earlier outputs as $name or getProperty('name')), an optional unit
  and an optional target, atMost or atLeast, whose probability is reported; control (inside an output) a control
  variate: input names an input whose own mean is known, or output names an earlier output with
  its known mean in expected; correlation a rank
  correlation, strictly between -1 and 1, between two inputs (induced by Iman and Conover's
  method); stop a sequential run, more trials until every output's mean has a 95% half-width
  within relative of itself, or max trials.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:monteCarlo" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:monteCarlo sampling="latinHypercube">
        <stochcraft:input name="labour" unit="h"/>
        <stochcraft:output name="cost" expression="$labour * $rate" unit="USD" atMost="7000">
          <stochcraft:control input="labour"/>
        </stochcraft:output>
        <stochcraft:correlation a="labour" b="rate" rank="0.5"/>
        <stochcraft:stop relative="0.01" max="1000000"/>
      </stochcraft:monteCarlo>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:agents, on a Scenario (after its ElementParameters and Calendars): the scenario is
  an agent-based model (ROADMAP M4.3, decision D16), with no process, and the file needs none.
  The scenario gives the run's length (its duration, which includes the warm-up), the
  replications, the seed and the time unit, which rates and intervals are in. agentType names an
  agent type and its state chart, a W3C SCXML 1.0 file named by a reference relative to this
  file (StochCraft's extensions to SCXML are in stochcraft-scxml-1.xsd). population is agents of
  one type: count created at the start and new ones arriving at arrivalRate, with optionally a
  network (kind empty, random with meanDegree, ring with eachSide, smallWorld with eachSide and
  rewire, scaleFree with initial and links, distance with width, height and range, links with
  link children naming two serial numbers, or file with the href of a CSV list of links) and a
  grid (rows and columns, Moore or von Neumann neighbours, wrapped or not, one agent per cell or
  many, placed in order or at random). environment names the agent type of one agent outside the
  populations. statistic is a count, sum, mean, min or max of an expression over a population's
  agents that meet a condition, recorded at the start, every saveEvery and at the end. stepping
  is the stepped mode: a step every `every`, the agents in fixed or random order. microstepLimit
  and retestLimit stop a run that loops.

    <bpsim:VendorExtension name="stochcraft:agents" xmlns:stochcraft="…">
      <stochcraft:agents saveEvery="1">
        <stochcraft:agentType name="person" chart="person.scxml"/>
        <stochcraft:population name="people" type="person" count="100">
          <stochcraft:network kind="ring" eachSide="2"/>
        </stochcraft:population>
        <stochcraft:statistic name="infected" population="people" measure="count"
            condition="In('infected')"/>
      </stochcraft:agents>
    </bpsim:VendorExtension>

  stochcraft:BetaDistribution, a BPSim 2.0 value (in BPSim's ParameterValue substitution group,
  like BPSim's own distributions), wherever a BPSim value can be: the beta distribution with
  shape parameters shape and scale (as BPSim's BetaDistribution reads them) stretched from
  [0, 1] to [min, max], its mean min + (max - min) shape / (shape + scale). min and max are in
  its timeUnit (by default the scenario's base time unit), and min is below max. BPSim's own
  BetaDistribution takes no other attributes and lies on [0, 1], so a beta on [0, 1] is written
  as BPSim's, and one on another range as this.

    <bpsim:ProcessingTime>
      <stochcraft:BetaDistribution xmlns:stochcraft="…" shape="3" scale="5" min="1" max="4"
          timeUnit="day"/>
    </bpsim:ProcessingTime>
-->
<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
    xmlns:stochcraft="https://helenkwok.github.io/StochCraft/bpsim/1"
    xmlns:bpsim="http://www.bpsim.org/schemas/2.0"
    targetNamespace="https://helenkwok.github.io/StochCraft/bpsim/1"
    elementFormDefault="qualified">
  <!-- The BPSim 2.0 schema, which a validator imports next to this one. -->
  <xs:import namespace="http://www.bpsim.org/schemas/2.0"/>
  <xs:attribute name="value" type="xs:string"/>
  <xs:attribute name="perWorkingServer" type="xs:boolean"/>
  <xs:element name="byShift">
    <xs:complexType>
      <xs:sequence>
        <xs:any namespace="http://www.bpsim.org/schemas/2.0 http://www.bpsim.org/schemas/1.0"
            processContents="lax" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      </xs:sequence>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:element>
  <xs:complexType name="Value">
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:any namespace="http://www.bpsim.org/schemas/2.0 http://www.bpsim.org/schemas/1.0 ##targetNamespace"
          processContents="lax"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>
  <xs:element name="BetaDistribution" substitutionGroup="bpsim:ParameterValue">
    <xs:complexType>
      <xs:complexContent>
        <xs:extension base="bpsim:DistributionParameter">
          <xs:attribute name="shape" type="xs:double" use="required"/>
          <xs:attribute name="scale" type="xs:double" use="required"/>
          <xs:attribute name="min" type="xs:double" default="0"/>
          <xs:attribute name="max" type="xs:double" default="1"/>
        </xs:extension>
      </xs:complexContent>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:element>
  <xs:element name="breakdown">
    <xs:complexType>
      <xs:sequence>
        <xs:element name="timeToFailure" type="stochcraft:Value"/>
        <xs:element name="repairTime" type="stochcraft:Value"/>
      </xs:sequence>
      <xs:attribute name="handsOnWhileDown" type="xs:boolean" default="true"/>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:element>
  <xs:element name="transferTime">
    <xs:complexType>
      <xs:complexContent>
        <xs:extension base="stochcraft:Value">
          <xs:attribute name="holdsSender" type="xs:boolean" default="true"/>
        </xs:extension>
      </xs:complexContent>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:element>
  <xs:element name="plannedRunTime" type="stochcraft:Value"/>
